From: Yanghan Ye <yyh94306@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 17:05:19 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] pinctrl: airoha: mux requested AN7581 pins as GPIO

AN7581 has two force-GPIO SCU banks at 0x228 and 0x22c. Unlike the
ordinary positive-polarity mux registers, setting a bit in these banks
forces the corresponding shared pin into GPIO mode. The Airoha SDK names
the ranges GPIO6--31 and GPIO32 onward. The stock XR1710G gtk_boot.sh
also sets 0x228 bit 31 and 0x22c bit 14 before using GPIO31 and GPIO46 as
RTL8261 reset lines.

The pinctrl driver currently resets only the first bank at probe and has
no gpio_request_enable callback. A GPIO consumer can therefore update
the GPIO direction and data registers while the physical shared pin is
not muxed to GPIO. This is particularly harmful for PHY reset GPIOs,
where a failed hardware reset changes the firmware and SerDes bring-up
sequence.

Describe the valid force-GPIO ranges per SoC, reset both AN7581 banks at
probe, and set the matching force bit whenever gpiolib requests a pin.
Do not clear the bit when the descriptor is released: the MT7530
pre-scan reset sequence intentionally releases its temporary descriptor
before PHY discovery, so the deasserted reset pad must remain in GPIO
mode until phylib acquires it.

The force bit overrides the ordinary mux registers. Clear it when
pinctrl explicitly selects an alternate function for the pin, otherwise
a released GPIO cannot subsequently become PWM, PHY LED, UART, or
another mux function. The stock XR1710G gcontrol.sh follows the same
rule when it clears temporary LED force bits while retaining the GPIO46
PHY reset bit.

Only AN7581 provides force-GPIO ranges. GPIO0--5 and GPIO47--49 retain
their existing mux handling, while AN7583 and EN7523 remain unchanged.

Signed-off-by: Yanghan Ye <yyh94306@gmail.com>
Assisted-by: Codex:GPT-5.6-sol
--- a/drivers/pinctrl/airoha/airoha-common.h
+++ b/drivers/pinctrl/airoha/airoha-common.h
